# MOVE_ANIMS, which Gen 4 animation a Gen 5 move borrows.
#
# <gen5 move>  <gen4 donor>   # <gen5 name> <- <gen4 name>: why
#
# A Gen 5 move has no animation script in this game, and the scripts it has in
# its own game are cell animations this engine cannot play. So tools/portmoves.py
# writes a Gen 4 move's script bytes under the Gen 5 id, one cartridge's member
# under another id, the same act as every recipe line, and this file says
# which. The two presentation flag bits (hides HP gauges, hides shadows) follow
# the donor, because they describe the animation that actually plays.
#
# `tools/portmoves.py --propose-anims` printed the first cut of this table by
# rule: same type and class, the nearest power for a damaging move, the same
# effect where a Gen 4 move has it, the newest otherwise. Rows marked "by hand"
# are where the rule was blind, it gave Hone Claws a sleep animation and Drill
# Run a move that buries the user, and every one carries its own reason. It is
# a first cut: anyone who plays a battle and winces at a row should change it.
